Just to chime in quickly, K and N--if N is the finite sample population size estimate derived by summing across all of the sites (I'm guessing it is here based on previous posts)--are separate things. The fitted model involves integrating over all possible values for each N_i (or N_i_t, for an open model)--K needs to be larger than any reasonable value for these. Exp(2.96) is about 20, and the population appears to be growing. In year two, you might expect (20*.88) survivors and 20*.15 recruits (i.e., something like 20.6 birds), so keep in mind that the expected value of N could be quite a bit larger if there are a lot of years, here.   E(N) could be also substantially larger than 20 for some sites * time-periods if there are some covariate effects that aren't being presented above.

